lima/gpir: one cycle of critical-path slack when picking the next node

What the blob does. The GP backend of libGLES_mali.so does NOT schedule under a
register-pressure constraint on its first try. Its driver loop at 0x000fc780-
0x000fcb38 is: schedule the block list (0x000f6954), allocate work registers
(0x000fa20c), compare what the allocation needed against what is available
(0x000fc990: ldr r2,[r7,#8]; ldr r1,[r7,#4]; cmp r2,r1; ble <done>), and only
when it does not fit lower the pressure (0x000fb198) and go round again, with an
iteration budget and a convergence guard - if the register count stops improving
for more than 3 rounds it gives up with "%s register allocation failed with
convergence for vertex shader" (string at 0x0010ede8, referenced from 0x000fcac8),
against the plain "register allocation failed" (0x0010ee2c) below that, and
"Insufficient MaliGP2 work register space" (0x0010ee60) when the allocation
cannot be helped. So its scheduler is free to pick for slot occupancy and is
corrected afterwards, where lima's single pass has to respect the 11 live value
registers as it goes.

Porting that loop is not what this patch does - lima's scheduler deliberately
avoids re-scheduling by modelling the bypass network as the register file - but
it does explain the one place lima is needlessly strict. try_node() walks the
ready list, which is ordered by sched.dist, and breaks out the moment the
distance drops below the best candidate's, so only nodes on the longest
remaining path are ever tried and the packing score (how many of a node's loads
fit in the same instruction) can only break ties inside that single tier. The GP
is not critical-path bound on these shaders - it issues 6 slots an instruction
and the Demo's heavy vertex shaders already run at 94-97 % slot occupancy - so
one cycle of slack, letting a node just off the path win when it packs strictly
better, is the cheap half of what the blob gets from its loop.

Demo GP instructions (= GP cycles of issue), one limacompile process per program:
  prog        1  4  7 10 13 16 19 22 25 28 31 34 37 40  43  46 51 | total
  blob       12 13 24  5 61 14 14 29 29 22 29 28 28 15 138 138 14 |  613
  0001-0016  12 12 26  4 64 15 15 29 29 28 33 36 28 15 115 115 11 |  587
  +this      12 12 26  4 64 15 15 29 29 28 32 31 29 15 115 115 11 |  582
Program 34 is the win (36 -> 31, 8 over the blob -> 3), program 31 one better on
98 draws a frame, program 37 one worse on 3. reference UI is unchanged in every scene
(Games/Clock 33, Photos 34, Home 49) and so is every PP count. No shader in any
captured scene spills (0:0 spills:fills for all 68).

Larger slack is NOT monotone and costs the shader that matters most - ignition's
program 13, 25 200 vertices a frame:
  slack    0   1   2   3   4   6   8  16  inf
  total  587 582 580 583 577 575 571 578 579
  p13     64  64  64  64  64  66  65  65  66
so 1 is the value, chosen for program 13 holding still, not for the total.

Picture risk: BIT-IDENTICAL, by construction. This changes only which of two
already-ready nodes the scheduler takes first; it adds, removes and reorders
nothing but GP moves, which are exact copies, and every arithmetic node keeps
its operands and its opcode. There is no arithmetic in this patch.

What this does NOT fix, measured so the next round does not chase it: ignition's
program 13 stays at 64 against the blob's 61, and it is not a scheduling problem.
Its final schedule fills 622 of 640 ALU slots (97 %); program 34's 340 of 360 and
program 31's 311 of 330. lima's packing is already near saturation, so the
remaining 3 instructions are 18 slots of WORK the blob does not do - move nodes
threading values through the bypass network, of which program 13's schedule has
60 in the pass slot and 50 in the complex slot. That is register pressure and
instruction selection, not the scheduler; the varying half of it is what 0012 and
0013 (FP16 mediump varyings, stride 8) already took from 32 bytes a vertex to 16.

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/gp/scheduler.c b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/gp/scheduler.c
index c4700dc..3f63daa 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/gp/scheduler.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/gp/scheduler.c
@@ -1285,6 +1285,22 @@ static void ASSERTED verify_max_nodes(sched_ctx *ctx)
    assert(ctx->instr->alu_non_cplx_slot_free >= alu_num_slot_needed_by_max + alu_num_slot_needed_by_non_cplx_store);
 }
 
+/* How far below the best critical-path distance try_node() still considers a
+ * candidate. The ready list is ordered by sched.dist, so 0 - what this was -
+ * means only the nodes on the longest remaining path are ever tried, and the
+ * packing score (how many of a node's loads fit in the same instruction) only
+ * ever breaks ties inside that one tier. One cycle of slack lets the scheduler
+ * take a node just off the critical path when it packs strictly better, which
+ * is what the GP's 6 slots per instruction want: the critical path of a GP
+ * vertex shader is not what bounds it, slot occupancy is.
+ *
+ * Measured on the Demo's 17 programs (one limacompile process per program);
+ * larger values are not monotone and cost the heaviest shader, program 13:
+ *   slack  0   1   2   3   4   6   8  16  inf     program 13 (25 200 vertices
+ *   total 587 582 580 583 577 575 571 578 579     a frame): 64 64 64 64 64 66 65 65 66
+ */
+#define GPIR_SCHED_DIST_SLACK 1
+
 static bool try_node(sched_ctx *ctx)
 {
    gpir_node *best_node = NULL;
@@ -1301,7 +1317,7 @@ static bool try_node(sched_ctx *ctx)
         &node->list != &ctx->ready_list;
         node = list_entry(node->list.next, gpir_node, list)) {
       if (best_score != INT_MIN) {
-         if (node->sched.dist < best_node->sched.dist)
+         if (node->sched.dist < best_node->sched.dist - GPIR_SCHED_DIST_SLACK)
             break;
       }
 
